module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.authored_quotation_terminates_the_string_it_is_authored_in

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data authored_quotation_terminates_the_string_it_is_authored_in: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "authored_quotation_terminates_the_string_it_is_authored_in" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a receipt that QUOTES a phrase terminates the string it is authored in, so the row filing the evidence is the row the evidence disables** (INVALID STATE: an authored ledger string containing a bare double quote, which the lexer reads as the string's terminator; the remainder of the sentence is then parsed as program text and the module refuses). The `.dag` lexer has NO STRING ESCAPING, so a quoted phrase is not expressible inside a string at all -- it is avoided by convention. The refusal names the first words INSIDE the quotation as undefined variables, which is a diagnostic pointing at text the author never intended as code, several hundred columns from anything they were thinking about. ",

    "HARM, AND IT IS NOT THE PARSE ERROR. A ledger row is an AUTHORITY whose markdown is a PROJECTION. An unparseable authority does not empty the projection -- it makes the projector refuse, and the committed markdown simply stays at its previous vintage while presenting as current. So the observable outcome of filing a receipt this way is that the receipt does not appear, every earlier receipt still does, and the file's own header still says it is generated from the authority. Nothing in the rendered artifact distinguishes `this row has no new receipt` from `this row stopped compiling`. The injury is therefore to the LEDGER'S CREDIBILITY as a record, not to the build: readers downstream take the file as the current state of a class, and it is silently one commit behind on exactly the rows most recently worked. ",

    "MEASURED 2026-09-07 (import-deletion lane, namespace cut NS-N), as a real un-staged occurrence rather than a constructed one. `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.unresolved_callee_conflated_with_dispatched_elsewhere`, committed at b1c6378c6d, carried the sentence `a wall of the form` followed by a bare-double-quoted predicate `unresolved AND some declaration shares this leaf` followed by `was proposed`. The row refused with `undefined variable 'this'` and `undefined variable 'leaf'` -- the first two bare identifiers after the terminating quote. It was unparseable for ONE COMMIT and repaired at 65ed6c4fe0 by respelling the quotation with backticks. SEVERITY IS DELIBERATELY NOT OVERCLAIMED: one commit, found and repaired inside the same lane. The row is about the class, not the incident. ",

    "**IT WAS FOUND ONLY BY RUNNING THE PROJECTION, AND THAT IS THE LOAD-BEARING FACT ABOUT DETECTION.** Reading the row does not surface it: the text reads correctly, and the break is invisible to a human eye and to grep alike. It was not looked for -- the projector was being run to regenerate the doc for an unrelated append, and it refused. So the detector, on the day, was a side effect of doing something else, which is not a detector. ",

    "**THE DETECTOR GAP AND THE INJURY ARE DIFFERENT FACTS AND THIS ROW IS ONLY THE SECOND.** That doc projections are gated NOWHERE -- an unbuildable or stale ledger is red in no lane -- is its own class, owned elsewhere and under repair. THIS row is a property of the AUTHORING CHANNEL and SURVIVES THAT GATE LANDING: a gate makes the break loud within one commit, which is a real and sufficient improvement to detection, and it does not make the state unwritable. An author will still write the quotation, and will still be told about it afterwards by a machine rather than prevented from expressing it. ",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable, and only once the projection gate exists -- before it, the class sits below the ladder for the reader, because the rendered artifact answers `current` for a row that stopped compiling, which is a fabricated plausible output rather than a low rung. With the gate, the failure is loud, located and one commit deep, which is honest mitigation. ",

    "CEILING: structurally impossible, and it is NOT reachable by diligence. The invalid state is `a quotation the author intends as text is read as syntax`, and it has a constructor today only because the language provides no way to write the intent. Once a string can CARRY a quotation, the sentence the author means is expressible directly and the failure has nothing to be built from. ",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY RATHER THAN AN ARTIFACT: STRING ESCAPING IN THE `.dag` LEXER, sufficient to make a double quote EXPRESSIBLE INSIDE A STRING LITERAL -- not a lint, not a checked-in convention, not a projection gate, each of which leaves the state writable and merely reports it. Until that capability exists the class CANNOT CLIMB PAST VALIDATION, and this row says so rather than presenting backtick discipline as a wall. ",

    "**THE STRONGEST EVIDENCE THAT CONVENTION IS THE WRONG INSTRUMENT IS IN THE SAME COMMIT AS THE BREAK, AND IT POINTS THE OTHER WAY.** Two files in the roster spelled an apostrophe as a DOUBLED apostrophe -- seven occurrences, `row''s`, `class''s` and the like, ALL SEVEN IN TEXT AUTHORED BY THIS LANE -- defending against a hazard THAT DOES NOT EXIST. An apostrophe inside a double-quoted string is ordinary text, and the corpus is unanimous: a scan of `dag/gunbc/recurring_failure_mode/` for the possessive forms finds them written plainly everywhere else, in the dozens for the commonest words, with no instance anywhere of the hazard being defended against. The seven are repaired in the same commit that files this row, because leaving them would be this row complaining in prose about a defect it was still carrying; the count is recorded here rather than the state, since the state is now zero and a receipt that reads `two files currently do this` would go stale the moment it was true. So one author, in one file, on one night, defended the case that was safe and did not defend the case that was fatal. The author's escaping model was wrong in BOTH DIRECTIONS SIMULTANEOUSLY, and the cost of the wrong-way error is a permanent cosmetic artifact in the rendered ledger. A convention that is misapplied in both directions by a careful author is not being held by diligence; it is being guessed at. ",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: when a receipt, ledger row, or any other authored string needs to QUOTE something, ask what the lexer does with the quotation before asking whether the sentence is well written. The tell at review time is a rendered artifact whose newest row is missing while its older rows are intact -- read that as `the authority stopped compiling`, never as `the author did not file`. The tell at authoring time is reaching for an escape and not knowing whether the language has one. ",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
